            {"question":"The resting tremor of Parkinson's disease initially starts bilaterally.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False"},
            {"question":"A pill-rolling movement is characteristic of Parkinson's resting tremor.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True"},
            {"question":"Muscle rigidity occurs when the arm, leg, or neck becomes stiff when moved.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True"},
            {"question":"Which finding is MOST characteristic of bradykinesia?","options":["Rapid voluntary movement","Slowness of movement with reduced spontaneous activity","Tremor occurring only during sleep","Increased muscle strength"],"answer":"Slowness of movement with reduced spontaneous activity"},
            {"question":"A patient with Parkinson's disease has difficulty getting up from a chair, turning in bed, and initiating walking. These findings are primarily related to:","options":["Muscle rigidity","Bradykinesia","Resting tremor","Dementia"],"answer":"Bradykinesia"},
            {"question":"Which handwriting change is specifically associated with bradykinesia?","options":["Macrographia","Micrographia","Dysgraphia","Agraphia"],"answer":"Micrographia"},
            {"question":"A patient with Parkinson's disease walks with slow steps and demonstrates reduced arm swing. Which cardinal feature BEST explains these findings?","options":["Bradykinesia","Resting tremor","Postural instability","Muscle rigidity"],"answer":"Bradykinesia"},
